      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Spackle is not one product. It is at least four, and the difference between them is shrinkage — how much the filler pulls back as it dries, and therefore whether your repair stays flat.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <h2 id="types" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          1. The Four Types Explained
        </h2>

        <p>
          Walk the aisle and you will find a dozen tubs that all say "spackling compound" on the front. They are not interchangeable, and the differences are worth two minutes of your attention because they determine whether you fill a hole once or three times.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Lightweight spackle</strong> is a vinyl or acrylic binder loaded with tiny hollow microspheres — glass or ceramic bubbles that take up volume without adding mass. Because so much of the tub is air rather than water, there is very little to evaporate, so it barely shrinks. It sands almost effortlessly. It is also soft, and it has no strength at all across a gap.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Vinyl spackle</strong> is the traditional formulation: heavier, stronger, and buildable in layers. It shrinks more than lightweight, so deeper fills want two passes, but it holds an edge better and is more durable once painted. On trim and corners that get knocked, it outlasts a lightweight filler comfortably.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Acrylic spackle</strong> sits between the two and adds a degree of flexibility. That matters on any joint that moves — around door and window casings, along the line where a wall meets a ceiling — where a rigid filler will eventually crack and an elastic one will not.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Epoxy and setting-type patching compounds</strong> are a different class of product entirely. They cure by chemical reaction rather than by drying, they have real structural strength, and they are what you use when the repair has to hold a fastener or bridge a void. They are also far harder to sand, which is why nobody wants to use them on a nail hole.
        </p>

        <p>
          For context on where spackle ends and other fillers begin, our guides to <a href="/joint-compound-vs-spackle/">joint compound vs spackle</a> and <a href="/spackle-vs-putty/">spackle vs putty</a> cover the neighbouring categories.
        </p>

        <h2 id="shrinkage" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          2. Shrinkage Is the Whole Story
        </h2>

        <p>
          Nearly every visible spackle repair failed for the same reason: the filler occupied the hole when it was wet and occupied less of it when it was dry. Understanding that one mechanism tells you most of what you need to know.
        </p>

        <p>
          A conventional filler is a solid binder suspended in water. As the water leaves, the remaining solids consolidate and the volume drops. In a wide shallow smear over a drywall seam that barely matters, because the loss is spread across a large area. In a deep narrow hole it matters a great deal, because all of the loss is concentrated in one small spot and appears as a dimple.
        </p>

        <p>
          Lightweight fillers solve this by replacing much of the water with air trapped in microspheres. Those bubbles do not evaporate, so the cured filler occupies nearly the same volume as the wet one. That is the entire reason lightweight spackle exists, and it is why it can fill a nail hole flush in a single pass when a heavier compound cannot.
        </p>

        <p>
          The trade-off is mechanical. Those same air voids mean there is less binder per unit volume, so the cured filler is softer and weaker. This is why the advice inverts with depth: shallow damage wants the low-shrink, low-strength product, and deep damage wants the high-shrink, high-strength one applied in layers.
        </p>

        <p>
          The practical rule that follows: <strong>under an eighth of an inch, use lightweight and fill once. Over an eighth of an inch, either use a no-shrink elastomeric formulation or accept that you are doing two coats.</strong> Trying to fill a deep hole in one pass with a shrinking compound is the most common self-inflicted wound in wall repair.
        </p>


