        <h2 id="by-job" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          4. Which One for Which Hole
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Nail and Pin Holes</h3>
        <p>
          Lightweight vinyl, one pass, flush. This is the easiest repair in the house and the one people most often overcomplicate. Press the filler in with a fingertip or a flexible knife, draw it off level with the wall, and leave it. Do not mound it up; there is nothing to sand back if you fill flush in the first place.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Picture Hangers and Wall Anchors</h3>
        <p>
          A removed plastic anchor leaves a ragged hole with a raised lip of torn paper. Push the lip in with the back of a knife handle first so nothing stands proud of the surface, then fill. If the hole is deeper than a quarter inch, use a no-shrink elastomeric filler or build it in two layers.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Dents and Dings</h3>
        <p>
          Shallow and wide is the easiest case for any filler, because shrinkage is spread over a large area. Feather the edges out well past the damage — a repair that ends abruptly casts a visible line under sidelighting even when it is perfectly flat.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Screw Pops</h3>
        <p>
          Fix the fastener before you fix the surface. Drive a new screw into the stud about an inch from the popped one, then either drive the original below the surface or remove it entirely. Fill both dimples. Skipping this means the screw pops again and takes your repair with it.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Holes Above Half an Inch</h3>
        <p>
          Past this point spackle is the wrong tool. There is nothing behind the hole to support the filler, and a fill with no backing cracks out. Use a self-adhesive mesh patch or a California patch, then skim with joint compound. This is a drywall repair, not a spackle repair, and treating it as the latter is why so many of them fail.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Painted Trim and Casings</h3>
        <p>
          An acrylic or vinyl spackle with some flexibility, because trim joints move with the seasons. A rigid lightweight filler in the mitre of a door casing will show a hairline crack by the following winter. If the trim will be stained rather than painted, spackle is the wrong product entirely — use a wood filler.
        </p>

        <h2 id="applying" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          5. Getting an Invisible Repair
        </h2>

        <p>
          <strong>Clean the hole first.</strong> Loose paper, dust and crumbs of old filler all prevent adhesion. A quick pass with a brush or a damp cloth, then let it dry.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Press, do not smear.</strong> Force the filler into the hole so it makes contact all the way to the back, then draw the knife across at a low angle to level it. Filler that has merely been wiped across the surface bridges the hole rather than filling it, and it collapses when sanded.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Fill flush, not proud.</strong> The mound-and-sand habit is the single biggest cause of visible patches. Sanding a raised fill inevitably scuffs the paper face of the drywall around it, and that scuffed ring absorbs paint differently — the halo you see afterwards is abraded paper, not filler.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Judge dryness by colour, not by clock.</strong> The whole patch should be a uniform shade, including the centre. A ring of dry edge around a darker core means the interior is still wet and sanding will drag it out.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Scuff, do not sand.</strong> For a flush fill, a folded piece of 220-grit paper or even a damp sponge is enough to knock down any slight ridge. Reserve real sanding for repairs that genuinely stand proud.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Check it in raking light.</strong> Hold a torch flat against the wall and look along the surface. Every defect that will show after painting is visible this way and almost none of them are visible under ordinary room lighting. Fixing what you find now is far easier than fixing it through a topcoat.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Tools Worth Having</h3>

        <p>
          A flexible putty knife in two widths covers almost everything — a narrow one around an inch and a half for pressing filler into holes, and a wider four or six inch blade for drawing off dents and feathering edges. Stiff blades are made for scraping rather than for filling work; a flexible blade follows the slight curvature of a wall and leaves a flush surface rather than bridging across it.
        </p>

        <p>
          Beyond that, a sanding sponge in a fine grit outperforms paper on a wall because it conforms slightly and is far less likely to gouge. Keep a damp cloth to hand for wiping filler off the surrounding paint while it is still wet, which is much easier than sanding it off later.
        </p>

        <h2 id="priming" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          6. Why Patches Flash Through Paint
        </h2>

        <p>
          You fill the holes, sand them flat, paint the wall, and in certain light every repair is still visible as a dull spot. The surface is genuinely level, so what is going on?
        </p>

        <p>
          The answer is porosity rather than geometry. Cured spackle is far more absorbent than the aged, painted wall around it. When the topcoat goes on, the paint over the patch soaks in while the paint over the existing film sits on the surface. The patch ends up with less binder at the surface and therefore a lower sheen. Painters call this flashing, and it is more pronounced the higher the sheen of the paint — invisible in flat, obvious in semi-gloss.
        </p>

        <p>
          The fix takes seconds: a dab of primer on each patch, or a product with primer built in. Primer seals the porous filler so the topcoat behaves the same everywhere. On a wall with many repairs, or any wall in eggshell or above, priming the entire surface is worth the extra twenty minutes.
        </p>

        <p>
          One related point people discover the hard way: a patch that has been primed and painted still needs the same number of topcoats as the rest of the wall. Spot-painting a repair and hoping it blends produces a visible patch of its own, in a slightly different shade, because the new paint has not weathered. Cut in and roll the full wall, corner to corner.
        </p>


        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>
          <div class="space-y-4">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Overfilling and sanding back</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">The instinct is to mound it up and sand flat. Sanding a proud patch scuffs the surrounding paper face and creates a halo that shows through the topcoat. Fill flush, and add a second thin coat if it sinks.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Using lightweight spackle for deep damage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Lightweight fillers are aerated to resist shrinking, which also makes them soft. In a deep hole they have no structural strength and will crack or push in the first time something knocks them.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Skipping primer over the patch</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Spackle is far more porous than the surrounding paint. Topcoat sinks into it and dries duller, so the repair appears as a flat spot in a sheen wall even when the surface is perfectly flat.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Sanding before it is fully dry</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Damp filler clogs the paper and tears rather than cutting. Wait for a uniform colour across the whole patch, not just the edges, and give deep fills considerably longer than the label suggests.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Filling a screw pop without driving the screw</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">If the fastener has backed out, filling over it just buries the problem. Drive a new screw an inch away into the stud, reset or remove the popped one, then fill both.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best spackle for nail holes?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A lightweight vinyl spackle. It is engineered not to shrink, so a small hole can be filled in a single pass without needing a second coat, and it sands with almost no effort. For holes under about a quarter inch it is the correct choice every time.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between spackle and joint compound?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Joint compound is designed for taping and finishing drywall seams over large areas — it goes on thin, shrinks noticeably, and sands very easily. Spackle is a filler for holes and dents, formulated to resist shrinking in a confined space. Using compound in a deep hole means several rounds of refilling.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Does spackle shrink?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">All of it shrinks to some degree; the type determines how much. Lightweight vinyl formulations shrink least and can often be filled flush in one pass. Standard all-purpose and patching compounds shrink more and generally want two coats in anything deeper than an eighth of an inch.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you paint directly over spackle?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">You can, but you should not. Cured spackle is far more absorbent than the surrounding paint film, so the topcoat sinks in and dries with a duller sheen. The patch then reads as a visible flat spot. A spot of primer takes seconds and eliminates it.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does spackle take to dry?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A shallow nail-hole fill in lightweight spackle is sandable in 30 minutes or less. A quarter-inch fill wants two to four hours, and anything deeper is best left overnight. Humidity and depth both extend it considerably, so judge by colour rather than the clock.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best spackle for large holes?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">For anything above about half an inch, a setting-type patching compound or a proper patch kit with mesh backing, rather than spackle. Spackle has no structural strength across a void, and a large fill with no backing will crack out however carefully it is applied.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use spackle on wood?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Only for painted trim, and only for small defects. Spackle stays relatively soft, does not take stain, and does not hold a screw. For stained wood or any repair that needs strength, use a wood filler or an epoxy-based repair instead.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my spackle patch keep cracking?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Usually the fill was too deep for one pass, or the substrate is still moving. A deep single application skins over while the interior is soft and cracks as it finishes drying. Build deep repairs in thin layers, and address the movement — a loose fastener or a flexing panel — before filling.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          Related Wall Repair Guides
        </h2>

        <ul>
