        </ul>

        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 rounded-2xl p-8 text-gray-200">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="mb-4">
            For the ninety per cent case — nail holes, small dents, picture hangers — a lightweight vinyl spackle is the right answer and the job is genuinely a two-minute one. Fill flush, let it dry properly, scuff it level, prime the spot, paint.
          </p>
          <p class="m-0">
            Step up to a setting-type patching compound only when the damage is deep or wide enough that shrinkage and strength start to matter. Using the heavy product for small holes just makes the sanding harder than it needed to be.
          </p>
        </div>

